'''''Platanus mexicana''''' is a species of [[plane tree]] that is native to Northeast and Central [[Mexico]] and [[Guatemala]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Know |first1=Gary |title=Mexican Sycamore – Platanus mexicana |url=https://nwdistrict.ifas.ufl.edu/hort/2014/05/20/mexican-sycamore-platanus-mexicana/ |website=ufl.edu |date=20 May 2014 |access-date=25 February 2022}}</ref> It is also known as the '''Mexican sycamore'''.

==Description==
The tree can grow as high as {{Convert|80|feet}} and has leaves that can be up to {{convert|8|inches}} wide.<ref>{{cite web |title=Plantanus Mexicana (Mexican Sycamore) |url=https://mswn.com/plants/platanus-mexicana-mexican-sycamore/ |website=mswn |access-date=25 February 2022}}</ref> It is a [[Deciduous|deciduous tree]] with leaves that turn a yellowish brown in [[autumn]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Platanus mexicana |url=https://www.public.asu.edu/~camartin/plants/Plant%20html%20files/platanusmexicana.html |access-date=2024-03-09 |website=www.public.asu.edu}}</ref> The leaves have five [[Glossary of leaf morphology#Edge|lobes]] that can grow up to 8 inches wide.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Platanus mexicana (Mexican Sycamore) |url=https://mswn.com/plants/platanus-mexicana-mexican-sycamore/ |access-date=2024-03-09 |website=Mountain States Wholesale Nursery |language=en-US}}</ref> When mature, Mexican sycamore can be identified by the coloration of its leaves relative to other sycamore species such as the American sycamore, though range overlap is almost entirely exclusive. The adaxial surfaces tend to have a darker waxy cuticle, the abaxial surfaces tend to be noticeably lighter with a fine carpeting of soft white hairs.

